{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you wanna make an omelet you gotta break\na few eggs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2550,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by omelet I mean a theory of everything,\nand by eggs I mean a billion billion subatomic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8380,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles obliterated in the next generation\nof giant particle colliders."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18360,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In June, the consortium of Europe’s top\nparticle physicists published their vision"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24590,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the next several years of particle physics\nexperiments in the EU."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28460,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A big part of that is the Future Circular\nCollider, which, if it happens, will accelerate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33900,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles in a 100 kilometer circumference\nunderground ring encircling Geneva."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38180,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’ll be nearly 4 times the size of the\nLarge Hadron Collider, and it would be capable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42960,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of colliding particle beams with 8 times the\ncurrent LHC energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47210,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The hope is that this will open the window\nto brand new physics - and perhaps break the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51710,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current deadlock in our quest for a theory\nof everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56100,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But do the FCC or other upcoming collider\nexperiments really have a chance of succeeding?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61660,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to discuss the incredibly\nambitious plans for future colliders, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66750,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "try to honestly evaluate their prospects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70190,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first, to get some perspective, we need\nto take a step back and talk about how collider"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74220,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technology got to where it is today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76340,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists have been building machines to\naccelerate and subsequently obliterate particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80860,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the 1920s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82460,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first was the linear accelerator, or linac,\nwhich uses oscillating electric fields to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87220,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerate charged particles in a straight\nline, while the beam is focused by magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91000,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cyclotron quickly followed - here the\nparticles are still accelerated by electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95840,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields, but now a constant magnetic field\ncauses the beam to spiral outwards from its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99830,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "central source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101170,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once accelerated, the particles were typically\nslammed into a motionless target - often just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106480,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a slab of metal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107950,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some particles would collide with enough energy\nto be destroyed, and their energy would be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112340,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "released in the form of new particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115010,
    "dDurationMs": 3649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In those first collision experiments, all\nsorts of never-before-seen particles were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118659,
    "dDurationMs": 4941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observed allowing physicists to begin to map\nout the subatomic realm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123600,
    "dDurationMs": 6379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a serious limit to the energy you\ncan muster by colliding particles into a stationary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129979,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130979,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To be precise, collision energy with a fixed\ntarget goes up with the square root of accelerator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136650,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137650,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means you’re wasting lots of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139440,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, if you can collide two\nbeams moving in opposite directions WITH each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143890,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other then you get the full oomph of the impact\n- twice the energy in the individual beams."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149860,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key to doing this is to store the particle\nbeams in a ring so that they can be collided"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155490,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at your leisure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157230,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To that end, the particle storage ring was\ninvented by Gerard K. O’Neill in the mid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163610,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1950s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164610,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within a few years, an Italian group built\nthe first particle beam collider - the AdA,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169630,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or Anello di Accumuliazione. Apologies for my pronunciation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173940,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its four meter ring collided electrons and\npositrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177540,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Soviets quickly followed with their VEP-1,\nwhich was smaller but collided electrons with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183210,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one another to get a thousand times higher\nluminosity than AdA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188830,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In collider-speak, luminosity is a measure\nof the number of particle collisions across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193170,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an area over a time period."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195660,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More collisions means more chance of producing\nweird particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199050,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Americans soon followed up with a 12 meter\nelectron-electron collider with a similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204020,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "luminosity to VEP-1 but higher energies than\neven the AdA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209739,
    "dDurationMs": 4491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally we’d reached collision energies\nneeded to test predictions of the still relatively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214230,
    "dDurationMs": 2350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new quantum electrodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216580,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was huge in solidifying our understanding\nof the quantum world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220940,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Graduating from electrons and positrons, in\n1971 physicists started smashing protons together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226760,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at CERN’s Intersecting Storage Rings facility."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230390,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CERN does have a habit of extremely literal\nnaming."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234550,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since then, colliders have only grown in size\nand energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238090,
    "dDurationMs": 7450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Tevatron at Fermilab in Illinois was for\na while the largest collider at 6.3km around,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245540,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "operating from 1983 to 2011."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249250,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It generated collisions energetic enough to\nproduce the very massive top quark, and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254560,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enabled the discovery of the final Fermion\n- or matter particle - in the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260109,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This entire effort reached its pinnacle with\nthe Large Hadron Collider at CERN in Switzerland."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265669,
    "dDurationMs": 3571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It propels twin proton beams in opposite di\nrections around a 26.?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269240,
    "dDurationMs": 3889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "km circumference ring beneath the Swiss and\nFrench countrysides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273129,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The beams cross in four locations, where they\ncollide with energies of several terra-electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277949,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "volts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278949,
    "dDurationMs": 2551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is, for reference, a lot of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281500,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the crowning achievement of the LHC was\nthe discovery of the Higgs boson in 2012."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286990,
    "dDurationMs": 5449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The existence of the Higgs confirms our explanation\nof how the elementary particles acquire mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292439,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- which of course we’ve covered previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296059,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a sense this was the last missing piece\nof the standard model - the one remaining"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300580,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle that physicists thought MUST exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304030,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LHC particle hunters expected that to be just\nthe beginning - that their giant collider"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309650,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would go on to discover many new particles\nto take us beyond the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314559,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most highly anticipated were the particles\npredicted by supersymmetry - or SUSY."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319610,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an extension to the standard model\ndesigned to solve several of its outstanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324720,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325720,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most notably the hierarchy problem - the fact\nthat there’s a huge difference between the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330250,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strength of gravity and the other forces,\nand a huge difference between the measured"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334180,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "masses of the known particles and what we\nexpect their masses to be from quantum field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339309,
    "dDurationMs": 1221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory calculations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340530,
    "dDurationMs": 5909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Higgs particle in particular should have\nan enormous mass if our Standard Model"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346439,
    "dDurationMs": 2551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding is the whole picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348990,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact that the Higgs turns up at a “mere”\n100 times the mass of the proton seemed to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354270,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "indicate that something beyond the standard\nmodel was needed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358130,
    "dDurationMs": 4279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "SUSY solves the hierarchy problem by proposing\nsymmetric counterparts to the known particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362409,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Among other things, those counterparts should\nhelp cancel out the interactions of the known"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367320,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles with the elementary quantum fields\non which those particles live, eliminating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372270,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of their mass in the process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375090,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the supersymmetric counterparts do their\njobs most neatly if they have masses-slash-energies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380860,
    "dDurationMs": 5869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a particular range: between 100 and 1000\nGeV."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386729,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Large Hadron Collider reaches energy a\nfew times higher than the top of that range,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392310,
    "dDurationMs": 2979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so it should have seen such particles by now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395289,
    "dDurationMs": 6021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But after a decade of searches at the LHC,\nthere’s still no sign of SUSY."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401310,
    "dDurationMs": 2729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we find ourselves at an impasse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404039,
    "dDurationMs": 4931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Standard Model is complete, but there\nis no compelling hint of the next direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408970,
    "dDurationMs": 1259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410229,
    "dDurationMs": 1261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what next?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411490,
    "dDurationMs": 3479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it’s clear that the Standard Model\nis not the whole picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414969,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Besides the hierarchy problem, the standard\nmodel also doesn’t explain why neutrinos"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419020,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420020,
    "dDurationMs": 3459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t seem to give us a particle that\ncould explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423479,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are other anomalies like the muon’s\nmagnetic moment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427259,
    "dDurationMs": 4251,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, to get closer to a theory that\nunifies our understanding of the Standard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431510,
    "dDurationMs": 4689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Model’s motley zoo of particles and forces,\nwe probably need to achieve higher energies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436199,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- energies even closer to the instant of the\nBig Bang, when the forces of nature were literally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442599,
    "dDurationMs": 3301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unified, as we’ve talked about before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445900,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other clever ways to probe these\nenergies - for example using natural particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450580,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerators like the sun or supernovae or\nquasars or galactic magnetic fields, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455770,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continuously spray the earth with particles\nat higher energies than we can hope to replicate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461860,
    "dDurationMs": 6419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these ultra-high energy cosmic rays are rare, and to reliably detect a new particle we need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468279,
    "dDurationMs": 6301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to watch the result of billions of billions\nof collisions - we need very high luminosities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474580,
    "dDurationMs": 3309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bigger and better colliders may be our best\nshot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477889,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with better before we move to\nbigger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481139,
    "dDurationMs": 5631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The LHC is currently in a long upgrade process,\nwith the final result being a factor of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486770,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "couple increase in power - but more importantly\na factor of 5 increase in luminosity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491689,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This will be achieved by upgrading the existing\ncomponents with more cutting edge versions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496219,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- for example better superconducting magnets\nfor more precise control of more energetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501669,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beams."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502669,
    "dDurationMs": 7201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve been in the first off-line phase since\n2018, with operations set to resume in 2021, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509870,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’ll be another 2-year shutdown before\nthe new high-luminosity LHC comes online in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515360,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2027."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517630,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point of this upgrade isn’t primarily\nto access higher energies where new particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521850,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might exist, but rather to make the LHC much\nbetter at studying the current range."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527330,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "IF either SUSY or other very high-mass particles\ndo exist, then they may be actually a good way beyond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534470,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy range of the LHC."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536640,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so for that we need a bigger collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540750,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so we come to the Future Circular Collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543740,
    "dDurationMs": 7630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it goes ahead, it’ll hit energies of\n100 TeV - 8 times the current LHC energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551370,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually the FCC will be smashing protons\nlike the LHC does, but to start with it’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557190,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collide electrons and positrons with the express\nintention of making as many Higgs particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561990,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as possible. Okay firstly, why electrons and positrons?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565340,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well remember that the first particle colliders worked with electron-positron beams, and for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570070,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good reason: they are easier to work with\ncompared to protons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573230,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s easier to achieve the energies and\nluminosities to produce, for example, large numbers of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579260,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs particles in relatively clean collisions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583180,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why build a Higgs factory?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585120,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Haven’t we already discovered the Higgs?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586990,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Discovered, yes, but there’s still a lot\nwe don’t know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590700,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, at our current energies, we don’t\nknow how often it interacts with the superheavy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595210,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "top quark, a process that might contain hints\nabout new physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599330,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Higgs can also be used as a direct search\nfor new particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604250,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The FCC will eventually graduate to proton-proton\ncollisions which will open up the discovery"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610400,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space further."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611400,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Future Circular Collider will cost 10s\nof billions over its life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615410,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is this a good investment?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618260,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’ll certainly tell us more about the Higgs,\nand it may discover that elusive clue to take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622550,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us beyond the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624300,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However there’s no guarantee that any new\nparticles exist in the expanded energy range"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629530,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the FCC will probe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631840,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, you can’t know if you don’t\nlook."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634550,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Future Circular Collider is the priority\ndefined by the European Strategy Group."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640080,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about the US?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641670,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ever since Europe won the giant collider game\nwith the LHC, particle physicists in the US"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647370,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have focused on smaller experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649960,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "FermiLab’s Tevatron was for a while the\nlargest collider in the world, but it was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654330,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shut down in 2011 because it couldn’t compete\nwith the LHC."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658790,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since then the Chicago facility has reinvented\nitself as leaders in, among other things,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664450,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrino experiments - and when we visited\nFermiLab earlier this year we saw the linear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669210,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle accelerator that is under development\nto become DUNE’s neutrino source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674190,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the next big-ish US collider will most\nlikely be the Electron-Ion Collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680790,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It won’t be anywhere near the size of even\nthe LHC, but will have a more focused mission."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686860,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It will smack electrons into protons and other\nnucleons to probe the details structure and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692100,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interactions between quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694250,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The EIC is the priority program endorsed by\nthe National Academy of Sciences, and while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700210,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it doesn’t quite have final approval yet, if it\ndoes happen it’ll be at Brookhaven National"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705320,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Labs on Long Island, starting in around 10\nyears."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708610,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’ll be an order of magnitude cheaper than\nthe Future Circular Collider at an estimated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714630,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1.6 to 2.6 billion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717040,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s still a chunk of change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719850,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is it worth it to do this sort of fundamental\nscience, even though the immediate returns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724780,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the nation and world are vague?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727990,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, putting aside the fact that our modern\nworld is built on the technologies that came"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732530,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from this sort of fundamental physics research,\nI want to leave you with a quote by Fermilab’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737790,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first director, Robert Wilson, when questioned\nby the Senate regarding the value of the Tevatron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744060,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the US - and in particular whether it helped\ncompete against the Russians:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750420,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He said, “Only from a long-range point of view, of a developing technology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755500,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Otherwise, it has to do with: Are we good\npainters, good sculptors, great poets?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean all the things that we really venerate\nand honor in our country and are patriotic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767440,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768520,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that sense, this new knowledge has all\nto do with honor and country but it has nothing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774570,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do directly with defending our country\nexcept to make it worth defending.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779250,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Wilson was addressing the US Senate, so\nspoke of the nation - but the larger value"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786120,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this sort of work is to elevate humanity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788900,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether it happens now or later - I for one\nam patriotic about being part of a species"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794910,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capable of doing something like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797280,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of coordinating thousands of scientists over\nmany decades to build these crazy machines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802620,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can crack open the inner workings of\nspacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806470,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey, so as you know - filming a youtube show\nin space takes a lot of resources, and I just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811750,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to thank all our Patreon supporters for\nmaking it so much more doable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815590,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without you we’d probably have to film in,\nlike, low-earth orbit rather than deep space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821450,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gross."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822450,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today a special thankyou to Alec S-L,\nwho’s supporting us at the quasar level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828340,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alec, for complete transparency - we’re\nusing your contribution to purchase something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832200,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like 10^30 protons to inject into the future\ncircular collider - assuming it actually gets built."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838370,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We're currently piling them up as tanks of\nhydrogen in Switzerland with a postit notes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842990,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with your name."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844460,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If any of those particular protons happens\nto produce a previously undiscovered particle in the collider,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849990,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll be naming it the Alec S-L-ino."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852570,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey, it’s a bit of a long shot, but you\ndeserve the chance at glory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857570,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you for your help."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859410,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time we talked about the most\ndestructive, parasitic, and generally unhealthy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863740,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relationships between the stars - from novae\nto black widow binaries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868750,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's get to your questions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871000,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jason Carter asks a tricky one: when two black\nholes merge and share a single, warped event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876810,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon, shouldn’t there be a thin sliver\nof space in between that is not flowing towards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881130,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either singularity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883260,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually not so much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884760,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key point is that space doesn’t have\nto flow directly towards the singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889610,
    "dDurationMs": 4419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think of the case of the rotating black hole\n- the singularity is a ring, and relatively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894029,
    "dDurationMs": 3641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few geodesics - free-falling paths - hit the\nsingularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897670,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most pass through the ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899570,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the merging black holes, you\ncan think of space as more flowing towards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903370,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the center of mass - although it’s not quite\nthat simple."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906100,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Numerical general relativity calculations\nreveal an event horizon cross-section that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911260,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the pair-of-pants function - it looks\nlike cross-sections of a pair of pants moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915950,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "upwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916950,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They start separate, and the event horizons\nmove towards each other as they join, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921170,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at some point the event horizons touch and\nthen merge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925050,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the instant after that touch what’s happening\nat the seam?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928610,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The flow of space anywhere on the event horizon\nhas to be exactly perpendicular to the face"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934370,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the event horizon - so at the seam the\nflow of space is straight down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940080,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In principle there should be a point right\nat the center where there is no flow - if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944420,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you were a point-like particle you could just\nhang there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947640,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But everywhere else - including above and below that point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950460,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the space is still flowing inwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953240,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David Kosa asks how would we describe the\ninteraction of two merging stars of equal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957070,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass whose combined mass exceeds the Chandrashekar\nlimit?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959650,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For those who don't know - the Chandrashekar\nlimit is 1.4 times the mass of the sun - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965620,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the maximum mass of a white dwarf before crushing\ngravitational pressure causes electrons to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971140,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be pounded into protons to form neutrons,\ncausing the thing to collapse into a neutron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975750,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977080,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That happens in the cores of massive stars\nwhen they die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979970,
    "dDurationMs": 7170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about white dwarfs that gain\nmore mass after they form? Or that collide to have above that mass?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987140,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the limit still applies, but typically you\ndon’t get a neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992190,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to get a neutron star, you need a\nvery symmetrical, clean application of pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996660,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the white dwarf exceeds the limit by cannibalizing\na companion star, and also presumably if it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001690,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collides with another star, then the process\nis very assymetric and messy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006240,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead you’re most likely to just get an explosion as thermonuclear fusion rips"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011450,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the white dwarfs carbon and oxygen\ninterior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014440,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gives you a supernova and leaves nothing behind but a pretty cloud of gas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019730,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leandro asks how we manage to get such good\nsound quality out here in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024929,
    "dDurationMs": 5931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well space isn’t really empty - there’s\nan average density of one atom per cubic meter,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030860,
    "dDurationMs": 2789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so technically sound is possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033649,
    "dDurationMs": 3581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Really it’s about engaging your diaphram\nand projecting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037230,
    "dDurationMs": 2949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Vocativecase say’s they will miss Bahar’s\npainting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040179,
    "dDurationMs": 5931,
